### What is the difference between Comparable and Comparator?

| Feature | Comparable | Comparator |
|---|---|---|
| Package | java.lang | java.util |
| Method | compareTo(T other) | compare(T a, T b) |
| Who defines | The class itself | External class/lambda |
| Number of orderings | One (natural) | Unlimited |
| Modifies the class | Yes | No |
| Used by default | Collections.sort, TreeSet | Passed explicitly |
| Functional interface | No (has compareTo) | Yes (SAM = compare) |

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;

// Comparable: natural ordering by account ID
public class Account implements Comparable<Account> {
    private final String accountId;
    private final String holder;
    private final BigDecimal balance;
    private final LocalDate openDate;

    public Account(String accountId, String holder, BigDecimal balance, LocalDate openDate) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.holder = holder;
        this.balance = balance;
        this.openDate = openDate;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Account other) {
        return this.accountId.compareTo(other.accountId); // natural order: by ID
    }

    // getters
    public String getAccountId() { return accountId; }
    public String getHolder() { return holder; }
    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    public LocalDate getOpenDate() { return openDate; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return accountId + " (" + holder + ": " + balance + ")";
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.*;

public class ComparableVsComparatorDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("150000"), LocalDate.of(2020, 1, 15)),
            new Account("CUR3001", "Priya", new BigDecimal("75000"), LocalDate.of(2021, 6, 1)),
            new Account("FD4001", "Amit", new BigDecimal("500000"), LocalDate.of(2019, 3, 20))
        ));

        // Comparable: natural order (by account ID)
        Collections.sort(accounts);
        System.out.println("Natural order (Comparable):");
        accounts.forEach(a -> System.out.println("  " + a));

        // Comparator: sort by balance ascending
        accounts.sort(Comparator.comparing(Account::getBalance));
        System.out.println("\nBy balance (Comparator):");
        accounts.forEach(a -> System.out.println("  " + a));

        // Comparator: sort by balance descending, then by name
        accounts.sort(
            Comparator.comparing(Account::getBalance).reversed()
                      .thenComparing(Account::getHolder)
        );
        System.out.println("\nBy balance desc, then name:");
        accounts.forEach(a -> System.out.println("  " + a));
    }
}
```

```text
Natural order (Comparable):
  CUR3001 (Priya: 75000)
  FD4001 (Amit: 500000)
  SAV2001 (Rajesh: 150000)

By balance (Comparator):
  CUR3001 (Priya: 75000)
  SAV2001 (Rajesh: 150000)
  FD4001 (Amit: 500000)

By balance desc, then name:
  FD4001 (Amit: 500000)
  SAV2001 (Rajesh: 150000)
  CUR3001 (Priya: 75000)
```

### What are the Java 8 Comparator factory methods?

Java 8 made Comparators composable and readable:

```java
// Basic comparing
Comparator.comparing(Account::getBalance)                    // by balance
Comparator.comparing(Account::getHolder)                     // by holder name
Comparator.comparing(Account::getOpenDate)                   // by open date

// Primitive-specific (avoid autoboxing)
Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::getAge)                    // int field
Comparator.comparingLong(Trade::getTimestamp)                // long field
Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::getPrice)                // double field

// Reverse
Comparator.comparing(Account::getBalance).reversed()         // descending

// Chaining (multi-level sort)
Comparator.comparing(Account::getHolder)
          .thenComparing(Account::getBalance)                // tie-breaker
          .thenComparing(Account::getOpenDate)               // second tie-breaker

// Null handling
Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.comparing(Account::getHolder))  // nulls first
Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder())              // nulls last

// Case-insensitive
Comparator.comparing(Account::getHolder, String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER)
```

### What is the TreeSet/TreeMap equality gotcha?

TreeSet and TreeMap use `compareTo()` (or the Comparator's `compare()`) for equality, NOT `equals()`. If `compareTo()` returns 0, the elements are treated as duplicates – even if `equals()` returns false.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class CompareToEqualityGotcha {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Case-insensitive comparator: "abc".compareTo("ABC") == 0
        TreeSet<String> set = new TreeSet<>(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER);
        set.add("abc");
        set.add("ABC"); // compareTo == 0 → treated as duplicate!
        System.out.println("TreeSet size: " + set.size()); // 1, not 2!
        System.out.println("TreeSet: " + set); // [abc]

        // HashSet uses equals: "abc".equals("ABC") == false
        HashSet<String> hashSet = new HashSet<>();
        hashSet.add("abc");
        hashSet.add("ABC");
        System.out.println("HashSet size: " + hashSet.size()); // 2
    }
}
```

```text
TreeSet size: 1
TreeSet: [abc]
HashSet size: 2
```

### When do you use Comparable vs Comparator?

| Situation | Use |
|---|---|
| Class has one obvious natural ordering | Comparable |
| Sorting by multiple fields at different times | Comparator |
| Cannot modify the class (third-party library) | Comparator |
| Need reverse, null-safe, or chained ordering | Comparator |
| TreeSet/TreeMap default ordering | Comparable |
| TreeSet/TreeMap alternate ordering | Comparator |
| Lambda/method reference for ad-hoc sort | Comparator |

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: compareTo inconsistent with equals

```java
// WRONG: compareTo returns 0 for accounts with same balance
// but equals checks account ID
@Override public int compareTo(Account other) {
    return this.balance.compareTo(other.balance);
}
@Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    return o instanceof Account a && a.accountId.equals(this.accountId);
}
// TreeSet with this: two accounts with same balance → one is dropped!
// Fix: add a tiebreaker to compareTo
@Override public int compareTo(Account other) {
    int cmp = this.balance.compareTo(other.balance);
    return cmp != 0 ? cmp : this.accountId.compareTo(other.accountId);
}
```

#### Mistake 2: Using subtraction for compareTo with integers

```java
// WRONG: can overflow for large values
@Override public int compareTo(Account other) {
    return this.priority - other.priority; // overflow if values are far apart!
}

// CORRECT: use Integer.compare
@Override public int compareTo(Account other) {
    return Integer.compare(this.priority, other.priority);
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Comparable contract.** `compareTo` must be consistent: if `a.compareTo(b) > 0` and `b.compareTo(c) > 0`, then `a.compareTo(c) > 0` (transitivity). Violating this contract can cause TreeMap/TreeSet to behave incorrectly.

- **compareTo and equals consistency.** The Comparable documentation strongly recommends that `compareTo` be consistent with `equals`: `a.compareTo(b) == 0` should imply `a.equals(b)`. When this is violated, sorted collections (TreeSet) and hash-based collections (HashSet) behave differently for the same data.

- **Comparator.thenComparing prevents duplicate dropping.** When two elements compare equal on the primary field, the tiebreaker ensures they are not treated as duplicates in TreeSet.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the return value of compareTo?" – Negative, zero, or positive.
- "Can a class implement both Comparable and use a Comparator?" – Yes. The Comparator overrides the natural order when passed to TreeSet/Collections.sort.
- "What happens if compareTo returns 0 in a TreeSet?" – The element is treated as a duplicate and not added.
- "How do you sort by multiple fields?" – Comparator.comparing().thenComparing().
